+{"schema_version":1,"plan_id":"ui","node_id":"U1","attempt":2,"source":"david","source_ref":"docs/trace/ui/U1/loop.jsonl:59 steer(D3,pause2)","findings":[{"id":"I1","rule_id":"RULE-2","layer":"services","files":["src/web/compiler/git.ts","src/web/compiler/index.ts","src/web/app/explorer/ruleFacts.ts","src/web/app/explorer/commit/Commit.tsx","tests/web/fixtures/trails/replay.mjs","tests/web/unit/compiler/data-5.test.ts"],"root_cause":"Layer: the ui compiler. It is filed as 'services' because the schema allows only the API's layers, as D2 did. Symptom: SELL-1's spine on #/rules/SELL-1 is missing rungs #52 and #64 (QA step 24). The same two rungs are missing for SELL-2 to SELL-6, and #/commits/7d3f791 shows one Rule chip, SELL-7, where there should be seven (QA step 38). Cause: src/web/compiler/git.ts:46 turns the trailer lines into a map with Object.fromEntries, so a key that appears twice keeps only its last value. The type at git.ts:11 (Record<string, string>) can't hold a repeated key in any case. Evidence that the real trail has repeated keys: F1's domain green commit has seven Rule lines, SELL-1 to SELL-7 (docs/trace/build/F1/packet/trailers.txt:420-431, and the api green at :511-522). Those are the packet's pre-rewrite SHAs (727fcc3, 7b3ebb3). Their subjects match docs/trace/build/F1/walkthrough.md:57 (row 52, 7d3f791) and :69 (row 64, f6c64dd). I had no shell to run git log on 7d3f791 itself; the t12 tester's confirmation at loop.jsonl:58 is a claim. Downstream, the collapsed value is read at index.ts:157 and split at index.ts:165, which handles only the comma form. ruleFacts.ts:56 then filters the spine with commit.rule.includes(rule.id), and Commit.tsx:61-62 draws one chip per entry. Other readers of a repeated trailer: (a) Fixes-Finding, index.ts:167. Repeated Fixes-Finding lines are common in the real trail, so all but the last are lost: build/F1 packet trailers.txt:169-175 has seven lines, F6 :102-114 has thirteen, and F2, F3, F1b and F0b have them too. No U1 screen reads fixesFinding (no match in src/web/app). (b) The DATA-5 privacy scan, index.ts:154, scans Object.values(commit.trailers). Today an earlier repeated value is dropped before the scan and also never emitted, so nothing leaks. Once the fix emits every value, the scan must see every value, or a denylisted earlier line will reach trace.json. A separate cause, not filed as a finding because no U1 screen reads it: index.ts:78 parses only A<n>/<id>, so the real D<n>/I<n> values (F1 packet :115 and onwards) compile with round null. It is for U3. Why no test caught this: the fixture format can't express a repeated key. replay.mjs:48 builds the trailer block from a JSON object (README.md:14), and data-5.test.ts:121 does the same, so every fixture commit has at most one line per key. data-1.test.ts:80-81 covers only the comma form. Recurrence: new cause, so it gets a new fingerprint. It is the same class as D1/I1 and D2/I1: a compiler shape narrower than the real trail, and a hand-cut fixture that leaves the real shape out. The D2 round's check of 'commit parents … on real git: parse correctly' (D2.md previous_attempts) didn't look at repeated keys, so it didn't prevent this. Fix direction: the trailer reader keeps every value of a key, in order. Rule and Fixes-Finding are the union of every line, each line also split on commas. The privacy scan covers every value of every key. The single-valued trailers (Agent, Plan, Node, TDD) keep a stated rule for a repeat, never a silent last-wins.","fingerprint":"RULE-2|src/web/compiler/git.ts|repeated-trailer-key-collapses","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given a replayed fixture trail where the prepare callback makes a real commit, in the data-5.test.ts:118-124 pattern, with the message body 'Agent: builder\\nPlan: build\\nNode: F1\\nRule: SELL-1\\nRule: SELL-2\\nTDD: green\\nFixes-Finding: A1/I1\\nFixes-Finding: A1/I2' When the compiler runs Then that commit's rule is ['SELL-1','SELL-2'] and its fixesFinding holds both A1/I1 and A1/I2 (tests/web/unit/compiler/rule-2.test.ts). Companion DATA-5 test (data-5.test.ts): a commit whose first of two Rule lines holds the denylisted address fails the compile, naming the commit. Alternatives: let replay.mjs accept an array trailer value, written as repeated lines, in the red commit; or export the trailer parser and unit-test it on a raw body.","untestable_reason":null},{"id":"I2","rule_id":"RULE-1","layer":"services","files":["src/web/compiler/index.ts","src/web/compiler/test-sources.ts","tests/web/unit/compiler/rule-1.test.ts"],"root_cause":"Layer: the ui compiler, filed as 'services'. Symptom: #/rules/RENEW-1%2F2 Step 3 reads 'The trail names no tests for this rule.' (Rule.tsx:171-172), but the matrix says 30/30 (QA step 29). Cause: index.ts:254 matches tests with t.rule === row.id. The C# tests trait the parts, never the slash ID. Examples: Domain.Tests/RenewWindowTests.cs:27 'RENEW-1' and :65 'RENEW-2', and Services.Tests/PolicyServiceRenewWindowTests.cs:28/:58. The requirements row is 'RENEW-1/2' (docs/requirements.md:90). The API's own coverage gate (tests/Uinsure.Policies.Architecture.Tests/Support/RuleCoverage.cs:52, :113-123, Expand) reads a slash cell as its parts, RENEW-1 and RENEW-2, with the prefix carried over. The compiler has no such expansion. test-sources.ts:13 accepts a '/<n>' ID but never maps parts back to it. David's D3 ruling, 'Collect the parts', makes RuleCoverage's reading the one to follow. Why no test caught it: rule-1.test.ts:120 asserts RENEW-1/2 → [] on the requirements-shapes fixture, which has no RENEW trait (QA note 2). The test pins the gap rather than deciding it. Under the ruling, that expectation has to change in a red commit, with a fixture that traits RENEW-1 and RENEW-2. Recurrence: new cause, new fingerprint. It is related to D2/I1 (MATRIX-1|src/web/compiler/trace-files.ts|matrix-id-regex-drops-slash-ids). D2/I1's direction, 'one rule-ID shape across the parsers, including the slash form', unified